1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,700
I'm Jiajun Rong
from Privatschule Schloss Heessen.

2
00:00:03,240 --> 00:00:05,100
LinguaGraph makes
such differences measurable.

3
00:00:05,560 --> 00:00:07,020
Let's ask
a German AI model.

4
00:00:07,780 --> 00:00:08,640
What belongs to freedom?

5
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:12,860
It answers: autonomy,
rules, one's own goals.

6
00:00:13,320 --> 00:00:15,460
Now ask the same model
in Chinese about 自由.

7
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:18,940
It answers: space, boundaries,
what one is entitled to.

8
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:22,260
The same word —
but a different cognitive map.

9
00:00:22,760 --> 00:00:25,200
LinguaGraph makes
exactly such differences measurable.

10
00:00:25,580 --> 00:00:27,860
AI systems now serve
billions of people

11
00:00:27,860 --> 00:00:29,460
in dozens
of languages.

12
00:00:30,140 --> 00:00:33,060
But they are trained
mostly on English data.

13
00:00:33,520 --> 00:00:35,280
Does a model understand
justice

14
00:00:35,280 --> 00:00:38,700
in a loan-decision system
the same in German and Chinese?

15
00:00:39,240 --> 00:00:42,020
If not, users get different treatment
depending on language.

16
00:00:42,480 --> 00:00:45,800
But standard AI evaluation
only measures task performance —

17
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:49,640
not whether the model's value concepts
are consistent across languages.

18
00:00:50,180 --> 00:00:52,200
That is a blind spot.

19
00:00:52,740 --> 00:00:54,280
How do you measure
something invisible

20
00:00:54,280 --> 00:00:55,920
like a model's
concept structure?

21
00:00:56,380 --> 00:00:58,320
Key idea:
ask the AI itself.

22
00:00:58,900 --> 00:01:01,580
We turn the LLM
into a controlled test subject.

23
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:03,980
The same model,
the same five topics:

24
00:01:04,540 --> 00:01:08,500
justice, freedom, responsibility,
home, success —

25
00:01:09,140 --> 00:01:10,380
only the language changes.

26
00:01:10,820 --> 00:01:13,580
Since it's the same model,
language is the only variable.

27
00:01:14,080 --> 00:01:17,700
From the answers we extract
one concept graph per language.

28
00:01:18,180 --> 00:01:20,920
Our new metric,
the Linguistic Divergence Score,

29
00:01:21,360 --> 00:01:25,040
measures structural divergence —
over shared concepts and relations.

30
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:27,720
And we don't just output
a number —

31
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:29,940
we can name exactly which
concept components diverge.

32
00:01:30,420 --> 00:01:34,040
We extended the experiment to 54 models
from different providers

33
00:01:34,580 --> 00:01:37,480
(59 measurements) —
including eleven western ones.

34
00:01:38,020 --> 00:01:41,100
Across all 59 measurements,
the Chinese–German signal

35
00:01:41,100 --> 00:01:42,920
is statistically significant.

36
00:01:43,540 --> 00:01:47,320
Permutation test:
p < 0.05.

37
00:01:48,060 --> 00:01:50,600
And it's not random —
it's culturally patterned.

38
00:01:51,320 --> 00:01:53,760
German concepts emphasize
autonomy and rules.

39
00:01:53,760 --> 00:01:56,340
Chinese ones
space and entitlement —

40
00:01:56,340 --> 00:01:58,520
the cultural direction clearly beats
a random null model.

41
00:01:59,160 --> 00:02:00,500
The key control finding:

42
00:02:00,960 --> 00:02:04,720
institutional knowledge — like mathematics —
converges across languages.

43
00:02:05,200 --> 00:02:09,820
But honestly: this mathematical
convergence is only indicative —

44
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:14,460
after decontamination it collapses
from 0.52 to 0.99.

45
00:02:15,300 --> 00:02:17,740
A label artifact —
which we falsified ourselves.

46
00:02:18,120 --> 00:02:20,140
Cultural concepts, by contrast,
clearly diverge.

47
00:02:20,140 --> 00:02:23,820
Even the relations between concepts
are organized language-specifically.

48
00:02:23,980 --> 00:02:24,800
Honestly:

49
00:02:25,740 --> 00:02:28,020
Our human experiment
with 15 people

50
00:02:28,020 --> 00:02:30,120
shows, under between-subject
conditions:

51
00:02:30,120 --> 00:02:33,420
no language signal —
a design artifact, not counter-evidence.

52
00:02:33,980 --> 00:02:37,460
Nine English-containing pairs
are not significant —

53
00:02:37,460 --> 00:02:39,640
consistent with how English-centered
today's models are.

54
00:02:40,140 --> 00:02:44,400
And our operational threshold of 0.10
is a heuristic rule of thumb —

55
00:02:44,540 --> 00:02:45,660
not a validated boundary.

56
00:02:46,080 --> 00:02:48,720
This makes LinguaGraph
a new kind of AI audit.

57
00:02:48,720 --> 00:02:51,960
Before deploying a multilingual model,
a developer can check:

58
00:02:52,460 --> 00:02:55,780
Does my model drift on value-laden terms
between languages — and where exactly?

59
00:02:56,300 --> 00:03:00,480
For regulators it provides transparency,
as the EU AI Act requires.

60
00:03:00,920 --> 00:03:03,260
The output is interpretable —
not a black-box score,

61
00:03:03,800 --> 00:03:06,980
but a list of the concrete
concept components that diverge.

62
00:03:07,620 --> 00:03:10,040
LinguaGraph.
Visible. Thank you.
